## 17. Engineering Rules

Follow these rules throughout the implementation:

1. Never fabricate successful command execution, test results, API responses, or deployment status.
2. Never replace required backend functionality with hardcoded demo data.
3. Never expose secrets to the browser.
4. Never trust AI-generated content without validation.
5. Never trust a client-supplied user ID to establish ownership.
6. Never allow AI-generated proposals to trigger consequential operations without explicit authorization and appropriate user confirmation.
7. Never silently discard offline edits or overwrite conflicting changes.
8. Never claim offline support for operations that cannot actually work offline.
9. Never add a dependency without a clear reason.
10. Never leave critical interactions as TODOs or visual placeholders.
11. Never disable TypeScript or lint rules merely to make the build pass.
12. Never hide errors that indicate broken business logic or security issues.
13. Never generate a massive monolithic component when the feature can be separated cleanly.
14. Never store monetary values as imprecise floating-point numbers.
15. Never use fabricated vendors, quotes, or statistics as verified real-world information.
16. Prefer the simplest architecture that satisfies the actual requirements.
17. Preserve existing working behavior unless a change is justified and tested.
18. Keep accessibility, security, and testing part of implementation rather than last-minute additions.

